Introduction 
Most people these days often carry around a tiny handheld computer that can make calls and doing research etc. But it always wasn’t like that, for years Nokia was the leading phone company in the world. And for a long time, it was worldwide market share leader up until 2010 and then the iPhone had introduced itself. Apple revealed the new iPhone in January 2007, and it was described as 3 things in a single device and that included a phone, an iPod and an internet device. In 2008 Apple had unveiled the app store to the public which contained over 500 apps and by 2017 over a billion iPhone had been sold worldwide.

All this matters because it completely changed how a phone worked and what a phone is for. Phones stopped being handsets and became platforms that gave developers a place to build on, this was a big deal for enterprise computing. Nokia had seen this problem and in 2011 the company announced that they had begun plans to integrate windows phone as its primary smartphone strategy and to start a new ecosystem with Microsoft which taught the company that the market was more than just the hardware alone 

We talk about the shift from Nokia’s hardware led phones to software led phones reshaped how people live and communicate and how companies compete in the same market.

We are looking at this through 2 viewpoints societal viewpoint and how smartphones actually changed everyday communication and how we now access information online. The business viewpoint covers its strategies, competition and the app economy and how and why a market leader lost its place so quick. These viewpoints are very fitting as both companies went through the same change.

The remainder of our report will cover the historical development the societal analysis the business analysis an ethical reflection and a conclusion
